    {
      "id": "PR_kwDOL-xLQ88AAAABP8_Unw",
      "title": "feat(proxyd): consensus_out_of_range_wait for blocks just above consensus",
      "author": "zhwrd",
      "number": 806,
      "body": "## Problem\n\nIn consensus mode, websocket subscriptions and HTTP requests see different heads:\n\n- **WS:** `ProxyWS` connects the client to a single backend, so `newHeads` for block N fires as soon as that backend imports N.\n- **HTTP:** `eth_getBlockByNumber(N)` is checked against the consensus latest block. That is the lowest head across the consensus group, refreshed every `consensus_poller_interval`, plus up to one HA heartbeat when `consensus_ha` is on. While N is above it, proxyd answers `null` itself (#552, #769), and state reads get `-32001`.\n\nOn a 1s-block chain, a client that fetches each block right after its `newHeads` notification loses this race on nearly every block. One example is Blockscout's realtime fetcher, which turns the `null` into `404 Not Found`, doesn't retry, and leaves the block to its catch-up indexer. We saw about one error per block and blocks reaching the explorer ~45s late.\n\n## Change\n\nNew backend group option `consensus_out_of_range_wait`, default `0` (off). When set:\n\n- A request that names a block above the consensus latest block, by at most `consensus_max_block_lag`, waits until consensus reaches that block or the wait elapses.\n- It then goes through the normal rewrite path. Responses still come only from consensus backends, and a block that never reaches consensus is still answered as out of range.\n- Backends are chosen after the wait. Consensus can advance during it by dropping a lagging backend, and that backend must not then serve the request.\n- One HTTP request waits at most `consensus_out_of_range_wait` in total. That includes a batch, which is forwarded as several minibatches (`max_upstream_batch_size`, duplicate-ID groups). A request whose budget is already spent gets outcome `exhausted`. WS messages are forwarded once, so each may wait in full.\n- Startup rejects a wait that is not shorter than the server timeout (`timeout_seconds`, default 10s).\n- Blocks further ahead than `consensus_max_block_lag` can't be on any consensus backend yet, so they are answered immediately as today.\n- CL consensus groups are unaffected.\n\nSupporting changes:\n\n- **Out-of-range error:** the rewrite error is now `*BlockOutOfRangeError{Block}`. It matches `ErrRewriteBlockOutOfRange` via `errors.Is`, so existing checks are unchanged.\n- **Wait loop:** it rechecks the in-memory consensus state every 20ms and stops on timeout or when the client cancels.\n- **Log ranges:** the block scan probes past the first out-of-range block, so a log range with both ends above the head waits for `toBlock`.\n- **New counter:** `proxyd_group_consensus_out_of_range_waits_total{backend_group_name,outcome}` with outcomes `caught_up`, `timeout`, `too_far`, `canceled` and `exhausted`.\n\nWaiting was chosen over routing the request to whichever backend is ahead for three reasons:\n\n1. Consistency guarantees stay the same.\n2. It covers every method the rewriter handles, `eth_getLogs`/`eth_newFilter` ranges included.\n3. Routing ahead would need method-specific retry-on-missing logic and would still race the poller's per-backend head, which can be up to one poll interval old.\n\n**Trade-offs:**\n- A held HTTP request does not take a `max_concurrent_rpcs` slot, since that semaphore is only taken around the backend call. It does hold its connection and goroutine for up to the wait.\n- Over WS, a held message keeps its `max_concurrent_ws_rpcs` slot.\n- Server drain can take up to one wait longer.\n\n**Not covered:** `eth_getBlockByHash` with the hash from `newHeads`, since proxyd can't place a hash relative to the head.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `integration_tests/consensus_out_of_range_wait_test.go` (new config `consensus_out_of_range_wait.toml`) covers:\n  - the block is served once consensus advances mid-wait\n  - `null` after the wait if consensus doesn't advance, without hitting the backend\n  - no wait beyond `consensus_max_block_lag`\n  - no delay for in-range requests\n  - a batch whose elements are forwarded as separate minibatches (`max_upstream_batch_size = 1`) serves every element once consensus reaches its blocks\n  - three minibatches spend one wait in total\n- Unit tests for `highestOutOfRangeBlock`: it finds the max across methods and ranges (both ends of a log range above the head included) and leaves request params untouched.\n- `TestStartRejectsOutOfRangeWaitAtServerTimeout` checks the startup check, with an explicit and with the default server timeout. Further unit tests cover `BlockOutOfRangeError` (`errors.Is`/`errors.As`).\n- Not covered: a group of more than one backend in which consensus advances by dropping a lagging backend mid-wait.\n- Test run: `go test ./...`, `go test -race` on the new test, and `golangci-lint run` (v2.11.4) all pass.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n",
      "repository": "ethereum-optimism/infra",
      "createdAt": "2026-11-17T21:45:30Z",
      "mergedAt": null,
      "additions": 503,
      "deletions": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "PR_kwDOL-xLQ88AAAABPkT71g",
      "title": "fix(proxyd): relay WebSocket requests as decoded",
      "author": "Qumeric",
      "number": 805,
      "body": "The WebSocket path checks the method of the decoded request against `ws_method_whitelist`, then relays the client's original bytes. `encoding/json` matches keys case-insensitively and keeps the last duplicate, so `{\"method\":\"eth_chainId\",\"METHOD\":\"eth_subscribe\"}` passes the whitelist as `eth_subscribe` while a backend that reads the exact `method` key runs `eth_chainId`. That bypasses the whitelist and the limits tied to it.\n\nThis relays the request as decoded, as the HTTP path does. Fields the client omitted stay omitted, so notifications stay notifications.",
      "repository": "ethereum-optimism/infra",
      "createdAt": "2026-11-16T15:18:30Z",
      "mergedAt": null,
      "additions": 42,
      "deletions": 1
    }
